<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaintia Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1835 CE (approx.) · capital Jaintiapur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the Pnar people that ruled both the Jaintia Hills and the plains around Jaintiapur, known for its standing stones, annexed by the British in 1835.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Jaintia kingdom grew from chiefdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pnar-people">Pnar</a> (Synteng) people of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a> plateau, who extended their rule into the plains north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a>. Its early history comes from oral tradition and later chronicles, and the register's start date is approximate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings ruled the hills through local chiefs and the plains from Jaintiapur, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pnar-people">Pnar</a> custom with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> court ritual and worship of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>. Monoliths at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nartiang">Nartiang</a> and Jaintiapur mark royal and clan memorials. The kingdom fought and traded with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kachari-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a> and the Koch rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Accusing the court of seizing British subjects for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, the British annexed the plains and then the hills in 1835, deposing Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-singh-of-jaintia">Rajendra Singh</a>. A Jaintia rising against taxes followed in 1860–1862. The hills are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a>; Jaintiapur is in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaintia-kingdom.jpg" alt="Jaintia Kingdom" width="320" height="258"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_gazetteer_1907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1835 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaintiapur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rajendra Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Khasi-Pnar traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pnar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jaintiapur, Kingdom of Jaintia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1668&amp;focus=jaintia-kingdom" aria-label="Jaintia Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7122 1253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaintia Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7442 1509L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7442 1509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7442 1509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1668&amp;focus=jaintia-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

